WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.141
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Proficiency.

2
00:00:05.441 --> 00:00:08.433
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.733 --> 00:00:16.645
Unit seven: The Anthropocene. In this unit, you will discuss humanity's impact on the planet's systems.

4
00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:18.744
Anthropocene.

5
00:00:19.444 --> 00:00:25.913
Anthropocene means the proposed era in which human activity shapes the Earth's systems.

6
00:00:26.213 --> 00:00:29.002
Scientists debate when the Anthropocene began.

7
00:00:29.702 --> 00:00:33.144
Your turn. Say: Anthropocene.

8
00:00:35.844 --> 00:00:37.228
Anthropocene.

9
00:00:38.217 --> 00:00:39.845
planetary boundaries.

10
00:00:40.545 --> 00:00:46.352
planetary boundaries means limits within which humanity can safely operate on Earth.

11
00:00:46.652 --> 00:00:49.999
We have already crossed several planetary boundaries.

12
00:00:50.699 --> 00:00:54.198
Your turn. Say: planetary boundaries.

13
00:00:56.898 --> 00:00:58.422
planetary boundaries.

14
00:00:59.417 --> 00:01:00.545
tipping point.

15
00:01:01.245 --> 00:01:06.101
tipping point means a threshold after which change becomes rapid and irreversible.

16
00:01:06.401 --> 00:01:10.119
Melting ice sheets may pass a tipping point.

17
00:01:10.819 --> 00:01:14.005
Your turn. Say: tipping point.

18
00:01:16.705 --> 00:01:17.764
tipping point.

19
00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:19.983
mass extinction.

20
00:01:20.683 --> 00:01:25.086
mass extinction means the loss of a large share of species in a short time.

21
00:01:25.386 --> 00:01:28.709
Some biologists warn of a sixth mass extinction.

22
00:01:29.409 --> 00:01:33.025
Your turn. Say: mass extinction.

23
00:01:35.725 --> 00:01:36.970
mass extinction.

24
00:01:37.983 --> 00:01:39.344
geoengineering.

25
00:01:40.044 --> 00:01:44.958
geoengineering means large-scale intervention to alter the climate.

26
00:01:45.258 --> 00:01:48.500
Geoengineering raises serious ethical concerns.

27
00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:52.618
Your turn. Say: geoengineering.

28
00:01:55.318 --> 00:01:56.459
geoengineering.

29
00:01:57.450 --> 00:01:58.347
rewilding.

30
00:01:59.047 --> 00:02:03.728
rewilding means restoring natural processes and wildlife to an area.

31
00:02:04.028 --> 00:02:06.898
Rewilding has brought wolves back to parts of Europe.

32
00:02:07.598 --> 00:02:10.866
Your turn. Say: rewilding.

33
00:02:13.566 --> 00:02:14.637
rewilding.

34
00:02:15.650 --> 00:02:16.686
carbon sink.

35
00:02:17.386 --> 00:02:22.137
carbon sink means a natural system that absorbs more carbon than it releases.

36
00:02:22.437 --> 00:02:25.586
Forests and peatlands are vital carbon sinks.

37
00:02:26.286 --> 00:02:29.345
Your turn. Say: carbon sink.

38
00:02:32.045 --> 00:02:32.953
carbon sink.

39
00:02:33.950 --> 00:02:35.613
ecological overshoot.

40
00:02:36.313 --> 00:02:41.679
ecological overshoot means using resources faster than nature can regenerate them.

41
00:02:41.979 --> 00:02:44.583
Humanity is in ecological overshoot.

42
00:02:45.283 --> 00:02:49.119
Your turn. Say: ecological overshoot.

43
00:02:51.819 --> 00:02:53.319
ecological overshoot.

44
00:02:54.317 --> 00:02:56.757
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:57.057 --> 00:03:03.736
Rithy, had humanity acted in the 1990s, would we be facing tipping points now?

46
00:03:05.050 --> 00:03:08.620
Probably not so many. We'd be in a much safer position.

47
00:03:09.917 --> 00:03:13.542
Some scientists argue we've already entered the Anthropocene.

48
00:03:14.850 --> 00:03:20.147
Should we cross more planetary boundaries, a mass extinction may become unavoidable.

49
00:03:21.450 --> 00:03:25.574
Some propose geoengineering as an emergency solution.

50
00:03:26.883 --> 00:03:31.042
If it were to go wrong, the consequences could be catastrophic.

51
00:03:32.350 --> 00:03:35.499
What about rewilding and protecting carbon sinks?

52
00:03:36.783 --> 00:03:43.000
Far safer. Supposing every country protected its forests, overshoot would ease considerably.

53
00:03:44.283 --> 00:03:47.270
Grammar focus: Conditionals beyond the rules.

54
00:03:47.570 --> 00:03:50.730
At C2, conditionals go beyond the basic rules.

55
00:03:51.030 --> 00:03:58.036
Should we cross more boundaries, extinction may follow. If it were to go wrong, the results could be catastrophic.

56
00:03:58.336 --> 00:04:02.658
Supposing every country protected its forests, overshoot would ease.

57
00:04:02.958 --> 00:04:09.567
C2 users vary conditional forms for nuance: 'were to' signals an unlikely or hypothetical future.

58
00:04:10.550 --> 00:04:13.748
Now read and listen. The Age of Humans.

59
00:04:14.048 --> 00:04:19.786
Many scientists believe that human activity now rivals natural forces in shaping the planet.

60
00:04:21.083 --> 00:04:24.348
They propose naming this new era the Anthropocene.

61
00:04:25.650 --> 00:04:33.129
Research on planetary boundaries suggests that we have exceeded safe limits for climate, biodiversity and nitrogen.

62
00:04:34.417 --> 00:04:39.737
Should key systems pass tipping points, change could become rapid and irreversible.

63
00:04:41.050 --> 00:04:46.289
Biologists warn that current species loss may constitute a sixth mass extinction.

64
00:04:47.583 --> 00:04:52.915
Some propose geoengineering, but were it to fail, the consequences might be global.

65
00:04:54.217 --> 00:04:58.979
Others advocate rewilding and protecting carbon sinks such as forests and wetlands.

66
00:05:00.283 --> 00:05:05.441
Provided that action is swift, the Anthropocene need not be a story of decline.

67
00:05:06.750 --> 00:05:09.426
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:09.726 --> 00:05:13.966
A threshold after which change becomes irreversible is a blank.

69
00:05:14.266 --> 00:05:19.347
A, tipping point. B, carbon sink. C, rewilding.

70
00:05:23.350 --> 00:05:25.570
The answer is A: tipping point.

71
00:05:27.083 --> 00:05:29.141
blank you need help, call me.

72
00:05:29.441 --> 00:05:32.433
A, Should. B, Were. C, Had.

73
00:05:36.417 --> 00:05:38.823
The answer is A: Should.

74
00:05:40.317 --> 00:05:43.210
If the plan blank to fail, we would need a backup.

75
00:05:43.510 --> 00:05:46.618
A, was. B, were. C, is.

76
00:05:50.617 --> 00:05:52.639
The answer is B: were.

77
00:05:54.150 --> 00:05:57.578
Restoring wildlife and natural processes is blank.

78
00:05:57.878 --> 00:06:03.899
A, rewilding. B, geoengineering. C, ecological overshoot.

79
00:06:07.883 --> 00:06:10.034
The answer is A: rewilding.

80
00:06:11.550 --> 00:06:15.430
According to the text, what may current species loss constitute?

81
00:06:15.730 --> 00:06:22.344
A, a sixth mass extinction. B, a new ice age. C, a carbon sink.

82
00:06:26.350 --> 00:06:29.174
The answer is A: a sixth mass extinction.

83
00:06:30.683 --> 00:06:36.761
Now it's your turn. Part one: Is geoengineering a responsible response to climate change?

84
00:06:42.061 --> 00:06:48.309
Part two: Use at least two advanced conditional forms to weigh risks and alternatives.

85
00:06:54.609 --> 00:07:06.320
Only as a last resort. Were it to fail, entire regions could suffer droughts. Supposing instead that we protected carbon sinks and cut emissions quickly, we might avoid that gamble entirely.

86
00:07:06.620 --> 00:07:09.165
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:10.150 --> 00:07:14.834
Great work today! You learned eight new words and conditionals beyond the rules.

88
00:07:15.134 --> 00:07:19.667
Today's value is caring for nature. Care for plants, birds and our planet.

89
00:07:19.967 --> 00:07:22.341
See you in Unit eight. Goodbye!

90
00:07:22.641 --> 00:07:25.166
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

